  ],
  "features": [
    "Multiple blur types (motion, lens, gaussian)",
    "Batch image processing",
    "Customizable blur parameters",
    "Preview functionality",
    "Command-line interface"
  ],
  "challenges": [
    "Realistic blur simulation",
    "Parameter optimization",
    "Batch processing efficiency",
    "Quality preservation"
  ],
  "solutions": [
    "Implemented advanced blur algorithms",
    "Created parameter tuning interface",
    "Optimized batch processing pipeline",
    "Added quality control mechanisms"
  ],
  "results": [
    "High-quality blur generation",
    "Widely used in ML data augmentation",
    "Efficient batch processing",
    "Positive community feedback"
  ],
  "why": "The blur detector from the Infortrend vision work needed blurry photos to learn from, and real cameras only take those when nobody wants them. Blur Generator makes them on demand from sharp images: motion, lens or Gaussian, with the strength as a number, or varied by depth with a depth map.",
  "gains": "For anyone training blur detection or deblurring models: every sharp photo becomes as many blurred copies as needed, each with a known type and strength. With a depth map and a focus point it also fakes a portrait mode.",
  "flow": [
    {
      "id": "read",
      "label": "Read the input photo",
      "kind": "start",
      "detail": "--type picks the blur"
    },
    {
      "id": "depth",
      "label": "Depth map given?",
      "kind": "decision",
      "to": [
        {
          "id": "check",
          "label": "yes"
        },
        {
          "id": "plain",
          "label": "no"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "plain",
      "label": "Blur the whole photo once",
      "detail": "Motion, lens or Gaussian",
      "to": [
        {
          "id": "write"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "check",
      "label": "Check the depth map",
      "detail": "Same size, 8-bit, gray or BGR",
      "group": "Blur by depth"
    },
    {
      "id": "focus",
      "label": "Focus depth given?",
      "kind": "decision",
      "detail": "--depth_focus or a focus point",
      "group": "Blur by depth",
      "to": [
        {
          "id": "band",
          "label": "yes"
        },
        {
          "id": "layers",
          "label": "no"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "band",
      "label": "Measure distance from the sharp band",
      "detail": "The band itself stays sharp",
      "group": "Blur by depth"
    },
    {
      "id": "layers",
      "label": "Cut the values into layers",
      "detail": "10 layers by default",
      "group": "Blur by depth"
    },
    {
      "id": "amount",
      "label": "Pick this layer's blur amount",
      "detail": "Between min_blur and max_blur",
      "group": "Once per layer"
    },
    {
      "id": "zero",
      "label": "Blur amount is zero?",
      "kind": "decision",
      "group": "Once per layer",
      "to": [
        {
          "id": "sharp",
          "label": "yes"
        },
        {
          "id": "blur",
          "label": "no"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sharp",
      "label": "Use the photo as it is",
      "detail": "min_blur=0 used to crash here",
      "group": "Once per layer",
      "to": [
        {
          "id": "add"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "blur",
      "label": "Blur the whole photo",
      "detail": "Same type, this layer's amount",
      "group": "Once per layer"
    },
    {
      "id": "add",
      "label": "Keep this layer, add it in",
      "detail": "Masked to its own pixels",
      "group": "Once per layer"
    },
    {
      "id": "more",
      "label": "More layers left?",
      "kind": "decision",
      "group": "Once per layer",
      "to": [
        {
          "id": "amount",
          "label": "yes"
        },
        {
          "id": "write",
          "label": "no"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "write",
      "label": "Write the output image",
      "kind": "end",
      "detail": "Bad photo, made on purpose"
    }
  ],
  "lessons": [
    "Up to 1.2.0 a decimal lens radius blurred like the next whole number: 2.5 gave the picture of 3. It now lands in between.",
    "With the default sigma of 5, Gaussian kernels wider than about 31 px all look the same; sigma=0 lets the kernel size set the strength.",
    "min_blur=0 used to crash motion and lens blur by depth; a blur amount of 0 now simply means an untouched copy."
  ],
  "architecture": [
